WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.060
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book one.

2
00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:08.386
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.686 --> 00:00:16.227
Unit twenty-six: Writing an Abstract. Today you will report research concisely using the present simple.

4
00:00:16.527 --> 00:00:19.156
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:23.368
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.217 --> 00:00:25.253
Abstract.

7
00:00:26.250 --> 00:00:29.805
Abstract means a brief summary at the start of a research paper.

8
00:00:30.417 --> 00:00:33.635
The abstract is limited to two hundred and fifty words.

9
00:00:34.250 --> 00:00:36.809
Your turn. Say: Abstract.

10
00:00:39.667 --> 00:00:40.726
Abstract.

11
00:00:41.483 --> 00:00:42.484
Summary.

12
00:00:43.483 --> 00:00:46.319
Summary means a short statement of the main points.

13
00:00:46.917 --> 00:00:49.706
An abstract is a summary, not an introduction.

14
00:00:50.317 --> 00:00:52.992
Your turn. Say: Summary.

15
00:00:55.833 --> 00:00:56.811
Summary.

16
00:00:57.550 --> 00:00:58.493
Purpose.

17
00:00:59.483 --> 00:01:02.447
Purpose means the reason for which research is carried out.

18
00:01:03.050 --> 00:01:05.386
State the purpose in the first sentence.

19
00:01:05.983 --> 00:01:08.543
Your turn. Say: Purpose.

20
00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:12.331
Purpose.

21
00:01:13.083 --> 00:01:13.899
Method.

22
00:01:14.883 --> 00:01:18.218
Method means the procedure used to collect and analyse data.

23
00:01:18.817 --> 00:01:21.617
Describe the method in one or two sentences.

24
00:01:22.217 --> 00:01:24.347
Your turn. Say: Method.

25
00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:27.980
Method.

26
00:01:28.717 --> 00:01:29.567
Results.

27
00:01:30.583 --> 00:01:33.291
Results means the outcomes produced by a study.

28
00:01:33.883 --> 00:01:36.289
Report the main results with key figures.

29
00:01:36.883 --> 00:01:39.396
Your turn. Say: Results.

30
00:01:42.233 --> 00:01:43.292
Results.

31
00:01:44.050 --> 00:01:45.098
Conclusion.

32
00:01:46.083 --> 00:01:49.128
Conclusion means the final judgement reached after reasoning.

33
00:01:49.717 --> 00:01:52.436
End with the conclusion and its implications.

34
00:01:53.050 --> 00:01:55.923
Your turn. Say: Conclusion.

35
00:01:58.767 --> 00:01:59.779
Conclusion.

36
00:02:00.517 --> 00:02:01.413
Keywords.

37
00:02:02.417 --> 00:02:05.705
Keywords means terms that help databases index a paper.

38
00:02:06.317 --> 00:02:09.361
Choose keywords that researchers actually search for.

39
00:02:09.950 --> 00:02:12.300
Your turn. Say: Keywords.

40
00:02:15.167 --> 00:02:16.179
Keywords.

41
00:02:16.917 --> 00:02:18.045
Concise.

42
00:02:19.050 --> 00:02:22.036
Concise means expressing much in few words.

43
00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:25.764
A concise abstract omits background detail.

44
00:02:26.350 --> 00:02:29.153
Your turn. Say: Concise.

45
00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:32.989
Concise.

46
00:02:33.800 --> 00:02:37.799
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:38.650 --> 00:02:44.147
Visal, your abstract is four hundred words and starts with the history of education. What's the problem?

48
00:02:45.250 --> 00:02:52.500
It isn't concise. An abstract should present the purpose, method, results and conclusion, not background.

49
00:02:53.583 --> 00:02:55.339
How have you rewritten the opening?

50
00:02:56.450 --> 00:03:06.484
This study examines how peer tutoring affects reading confidence, and the results suggest a significant improvement after eight weeks.

51
00:03:07.583 --> 00:03:10.279
Why the present simple, when the study is finished?

52
00:03:11.383 --> 00:03:18.773
Because the paper itself presents the findings now. The procedure is often in the past, but the claims are in the present.

53
00:03:19.933 --> 00:03:21.747
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:22.583 --> 00:03:26.127
Grammar focus: Present simple for reporting research.

55
00:03:26.427 --> 00:03:27.660
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:28.350 --> 00:03:31.952
This study examines how peer tutoring affects reading confidence.

57
00:03:32.850 --> 00:03:36.220
The results suggest a significant improvement after eight weeks.

58
00:03:37.117 --> 00:03:40.347
The paper concludes that peer tutoring is cost-effective.

59
00:03:41.300 --> 00:03:51.627
In abstracts, use the present simple for what the paper does and what the findings mean (examines, suggests, concludes). The past simple is used for completed procedures (participants were interviewed).

60
00:03:52.383 --> 00:03:57.590
Be careful! Don't say: This study examined how … and results suggested …

61
00:03:59.533 --> 00:04:02.938
Say: This study examines how …, and the results suggest …

62
00:04:03.238 --> 00:04:05.934
Abstracts report the study in the present simple.

63
00:04:06.733 --> 00:04:09.270
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:10.067 --> 00:04:11.753
Two Hundred Words That Matter.

65
00:04:12.053 --> 00:04:16.676
For most readers, the abstract is the only part of a paper they will ever read.

66
00:04:17.667 --> 00:04:23.730
It must therefore convey the purpose, method, results and conclusion in a strictly limited space.

67
00:04:24.733 --> 00:04:32.039
Writers typically state what the paper does in the present simple: this study examines a problem, and the results suggest an answer.

68
00:04:33.033 --> 00:04:38.713
Background information and citations are usually omitted, since every word must earn its place.

69
00:04:39.700 --> 00:04:44.857
Carefully chosen keywords then ensure that the paper can be found by the researchers who need it.

70
00:04:45.967 --> 00:04:49.968
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:50.817 --> 00:04:54.024
This study blank how peer tutoring affects confidence.

72
00:04:54.324 --> 00:04:58.244
A, examine. B, examines. C, is examine.

73
00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:04.241
The answer is B: examines.

74
00:05:05.383 --> 00:05:08.555
Which tense suits a completed procedure in an abstract?

75
00:05:08.855 --> 00:05:15.110
A, Participants are interviewing.. B, Participants were interviewed.. C, Participants have been interview..

76
00:05:18.667 --> 00:05:21.583
The answer is B: Participants were interviewed..

77
00:05:22.750 --> 00:05:25.539
Which element is usually omitted from an abstract?

78
00:05:25.839 --> 00:05:30.270
A, the results. B, detailed background. C, the purpose.

79
00:05:33.833 --> 00:05:36.471
The answer is B: detailed background.

80
00:05:37.617 --> 00:05:40.127
Expressing much in few words means being blank.

81
00:05:40.427 --> 00:05:45.160
A, concise. B, verbose. C, abstract.

82
00:05:48.700 --> 00:05:50.444
The answer is A: concise.

83
00:05:51.583 --> 00:05:54.221
According to the reading, why do keywords matter?

84
00:05:54.521 --> 00:06:00.880
A, They help researchers find the paper.. B, They replace the conclusion.. C, They make the abstract longer..

85
00:06:04.433 --> 00:06:07.803
The answer is A: They help researchers find the paper..

86
00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:12.256
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:13.117 --> 00:06:16.335
Why is the abstract often the most-read part of a paper?

88
00:06:33.150 --> 00:06:34.627
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:34.927 --> 00:07:13.474
The abstract is often the most-read part because researchers use it to decide whether a paper is worth their time. Thousands of articles are published every day, so busy readers scan abstracts in databases before downloading anything. For example, a student writing a literature review might read fifty abstracts but only ten full papers. Some argue that this encourages superficial reading, and that concern is valid. Nevertheless, it means a clear, concise abstract is essential for any paper to have impact.

90
00:07:13.774 --> 00:07:15.495
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:16.383 --> 00:07:21.474
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and present simple for reporting research.

92
00:07:21.774 --> 00:07:27.526
Today's value is self-discipline. Control your habits, so your habits do not control you.

93
00:07:27.826 --> 00:07:30.815
See you in Unit twenty-seven. Goodbye!

94
00:07:31.115 --> 00:07:34.417
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

